\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\title{Preliminaries}
\date{2026-11-04}


\p{The notes assume a background in mathematical physics rather than develop one.
Differential forms, with the potential a #{1}-form, the field strength its exterior
derivative, and the sourced equations needing a Hodge star, are recalled in
\ref{fdip-00AA} and used in \ref{fdip-00AB} and \ref{fdip-00AD}. Clifford algebras
of a quadratic space, their even parts and the spinors they act on, are used from
Part V on, with the square-quotient convention stated in \ref{fdip-000P}. Gauge theory is assumed at the level of
connections, gauge transformations and the actions invariant under them. The
Euclidean formulation of quantum field theory is assumed as well: a law given by a
measure, a Hilbert space of states recovered by reflection positivity
\ref{fdip-007L}, and fermions carried by anticommuting variables. Geometric calculus is
not required, but a reader who knows it may recognise in these constructions the
unifications it presents, a single derivative acting through the geometric product and
fields of different grades in one algebra.}

\p{Where a law enters, the notes use the ordinary measure theory of finite-dimensional
real spaces: Borel sets, the Lebesgue integral, monotone convergence, and Gaussian
measures given by a density. The carrier stays finite, but the real numbers return at
the law, since a Gaussian law on real coordinates has a density rather than a finite
table of weights; conditioning, marginalising and comparing covariances are then
integrations, and a retained covariance is a Schur complement \ref{fdip-0020}. A
reader should be able to complete a square and to integrate an exponential of a
quadratic form.}

\p{The algebra is that of a commutative ring with identity, its modules and its
algebras. A \vocab{ring} is a set with addition and multiplication: addition
makes it an abelian group, multiplication makes it a monoid, and multiplication
distributes over addition; every ring below is commutative and has an identity
#{1_R}. An #{R}-module is an abelian group #{M} with a scalar multiplication
#{R\times M\longrightarrow M} that is additive in each argument, respects
multiplication in #{R}, and sends #{1_R} to the identity map. An
#{R}-\vocab{algebra} is a ring #{A} with a \vocab{ring homomorphism}
#{\iota:R\longrightarrow A} whose image is central, so that #{A} is an
#{R}-module under #{r\cdot x=\iota(r)x}. Those axiom lists are the algebra suite's
\vocabk{ring}{ca-000U}, \vocabk{module}{ca-000X}, \vocabk{algebra}{ca-0016} and
\vocabk{ring homomorphism}{ca-0014}, and a tree that uses a further structure links it
where it uses it.}
